\title{Emergent Spacetime from Quantum Entanglement:\\A Finite ER=EPR Toy Model}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 2, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a self-contained finite model in which the entanglement structure of a real pure two-qubit state determines a two-boundary geometry with one possible weighted throat. A state is represented by a real $2\times2$ coefficient matrix. Product states are outer products and therefore have zero determinant; concurrence is twice the absolute determinant. For the Bell state $|\Phi^+\rangle=(|00\rangle+|11\rangle)/\sqrt2$, direct contraction gives both one-qubit reduced density matrices as $I/2$, the concurrence is $1$, and no product factorization exists. Boundary entropies reconstruct a nonnegative throat by
\[
w=\frac{S(L)+S(R)-S(LR)}2.
\]
This is an exact inverse to the cut data of every one-throat geometry. The Bell entropy triple $(1,1,0)$ therefore reconstructs a unit microscopic Einstein--Rosen bridge. The construction is an algebraic toy model, not a statement about continuum gravity, but it isolates a precise mechanism by which mutual information can parameterize geometry.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The possibility that spacetime geometry may emerge from quantum entanglement motivates a basic mathematical question: what is the smallest setting in which boundary correlation data determine a bulk connection? A finite model cannot reproduce quantum field theory, gravity, or a dynamical Einstein--Rosen bridge. It can, however, make the proposed dictionary exact and separate three tasks: detecting entanglement, extracting local information, and reconstructing geometry from boundary data.

We study two real qubits and a graph with two boundaries and one possible weighted throat. The central state is
\[
|\Phi^+\rangle=\frac{|00\rangle+|11\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
\]
Its local density matrices are maximally mixed even though the complete state is pure. Its subsystem entropies are therefore one bit each, while the total entropy is zero. The mutual information
\[
I(L:R)=S(L)+S(R)-S(LR)
\]
counts the unique throat twice in the cut model, leading to $w=I(L:R)/2$.

Our main theorem combines exact quantum and geometric statements: the Bell state is nonfactorizable, has unit concurrence and maximally mixed marginals, and its boundary data reconstruct a unit throat. Amplitudes are real; the Bell entropy values are supplied as boundary data rather than derived within a general entropy theory; and geometry is a weighted cut graph rather than a manifold. These restrictions make the entire argument explicit.

\section{The quantum model}

\subsection{Coefficient matrices and factorization}

A real pure two-qubit state has the form
\[
|\psi\rangle=\sum_{i,j\in\{0,1\}}\psi_{ij}|ij\rangle,
\qquad
\Psi=(\psi_{ij})=
\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Physical normalization is $a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2=1$, although the first algebraic lemma does not require it.

\begin{definition}[Product state]
The coefficient matrix $\Psi$ is a product state if real vectors $u=(u_0,u_1)$ and $v=(v_0,v_1)$ exist such that $\psi_{ij}=u_i v_j$ for all $i,j$. Equivalently, $\Psi=uv^{\mathsf T}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Entanglement determinant and concurrence]
Set
\[
\Delta(\psi)=\det\Psi,
\qquad
C(\psi)=2|\Delta(\psi)|.
\]
For normalized real pure two-qubit states, this is the usual pure-state concurrence.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Product-state determinant]
Every real pure two-qubit product state satisfies $\Delta(\psi)=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $\Psi=uv^{\mathsf T}$,
\[
\det\Psi=(u_0v_0)(u_1v_1)-(u_0v_1)(u_1v_0)=0.
\]
Equivalently, an outer product has rank at most one.
\end{proof}

Thus a nonzero determinant certifies entanglement, in the precise sense of failure to factor as a product.

\subsection{Reduced density matrices}

\begin{definition}[Reduced states]
For a real coefficient matrix $\Psi$, define
\[
(\rho_L)_{ik}=\sum_{j=0}^1\psi_{ij}\psi_{kj},
\qquad
(\rho_R)_{j\ell}=\sum_{i=0}^1\psi_{ij}\psi_{i\ell}.
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\rho_L=\Psi\Psi^{\mathsf T},
\qquad
\rho_R=\Psi^{\mathsf T}\Psi.
\]
The maximally mixed qubit is $\rho_*=I/2$.
\end{definition}

The Bell coefficient matrix is
\[
B=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Bell marginals]
Both reduced density matrices of $|\Phi^+\rangle$ equal the maximally mixed state:
\[
\rho_L=\rho_R=\frac I2.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $B=I/\sqrt2$ is real and diagonal,
\[
BB^{\mathsf T}=B^{\mathsf T}B=I/2.
\]
The diagonal entries use $(1/\sqrt2)^2=1/2$, and all off-diagonal entries vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unit Bell concurrence]
The Bell state has $C(\Phi^+)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The determinant of $B$ is $1/2$, so $C(\Phi^+)=2|1/2|=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bell nonfactorization]
The Bell state is not a product state.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
A product factorization would force $\det B=0$ by the product-state determinant lemma, contradicting $\det B=1/2$.
\end{proof}

The determinant detects nonfactorization, while the marginals show that globally available information is absent from either subsystem alone.

\section{The one-throat geometry}

\begin{definition}[Two-boundary geometry]
A two-boundary one-throat geometry has labeled boundaries $L$ and $R$ and one throat with weight $w\in\mathbb R$, where $w\ge0$.
\end{definition}

The weight is an abstract cut capacity, not a continuum area, length, or gravitational solution.

\begin{definition}[Boundary data]
Boundary entanglement data are a triple
\[
E=(S(L),S(R),S(LR))
\]
of real values interpreted as left, right, and total boundary entropies.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Generated cut data]
A throat of weight $w$ generates
\[
S(L)=w,\qquad S(R)=w,\qquad S(LR)=0.
\]
Each proper boundary cut crosses the throat once, whereas the full boundary crosses it zero times.
\end{definition}

For data satisfying $S(L)+S(R)-S(LR)\ge0$, define the reconstruction
\[
\mathcal R(E)=\frac{S(L)+S(R)-S(LR)}2.
\]
The numerator is mutual information, so the throat weight is half the mutual information.

\begin{theorem}[Exact one-throat reconstruction]
For every nonnegative throat weight $w$, reconstruction from the cut data generated by that throat returns exactly $w$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Direct substitution gives
\[
\mathcal R(E)=\frac{w+w-0}{2}=w.
\]
Nonnegativity of $w$ guarantees admissibility.
\end{proof}

Thus the generated boundary data retain the complete geometric degree of freedom. Although three values are displayed, generated data lie on the one-dimensional ray $(w,w,0)$.

\begin{definition}[Microscopic Einstein--Rosen bridge]
A geometry is a microscopic Einstein--Rosen bridge in this finite model if its throat weight is $1$.
\end{definition}

The unit is fixed by measuring Bell entropies in bits.

\section{The finite ER=EPR correspondence}

The Bell pair has reduced states $I/2$. Their binary spectrum $(1/2,1/2)$ has entropy one bit, while the complete Bell state is pure and has entropy zero. We therefore assign the boundary data
\[
E_{\mathrm{Bell}}=(1,1,0).
\]
Only this explicit entropy triple is needed; a general spectral entropy theory is not assumed.

\begin{theorem}[Bell-pair bridge reconstruction]
The Bell boundary data reconstruct a unit microscopic bridge.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The admissibility expression is $1+1-0=2\ge0$, and
\[
\mathcal R(E_{\mathrm{Bell}})=\frac{1+1-0}{2}=1.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite ER=EPR correspondence]
For the Bell state $|\Phi^+\rangle$, all of the following hold simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the state admits no product factorization;
\item its left reduced density matrix is $I/2$;
\item its right reduced density matrix is $I/2$;
\item its concurrence is $1$; and
\item its entropy data $(1,1,0)$ reconstruct a unit-weight microscopic throat.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Nonfactorization follows from the nonzero determinant; both marginal identities follow by direct contraction; concurrence follows from $\det B=1/2$; and the geometric conclusion follows from the reconstruction calculation above.
\end{proof}

This is the complete content of ER=EPR in the finite model. The EPR side consists of nonfactorization, unit concurrence, and maximally mixed marginals. The ER side is the unit throat. The theorem is an agreement between entanglement data and cut-geometric reconstruction, not an identification of a Hilbert space with a spacetime manifold.

\subsection{Control example}

The product state $|00\rangle$ has matrix
\[
P=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&0\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Its determinant and concurrence vanish. Both marginals are the pure projector $\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$ rather than $I/2$. Its zero entropy triple $(0,0,0)$ reconstructs weight zero. In this canonical control, removing entanglement removes the throat.

\section{Algorithms and numerical exploration}

\begin{algorithm}[Two-boundary throat reconstruction]
Given real numbers $s_L,s_R,s_{LR}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item compute $m=s_L+s_R-s_{LR}$;
\item if $m<0$, reject the data as inadmissible for a nonnegative one-throat geometry;
\item otherwise return $w=m/2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

The method uses a fixed number of arithmetic operations and therefore has time complexity $O(1)$ and auxiliary-space complexity $O(1)$. Under exact arithmetic, the reconstruction theorem proves exact recovery on generated data. Floating-point implementations should allow a small tolerance near zero.

\begin{algorithm}[Two-qubit diagnostics]
Given $\Psi=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, compute
\[
\Delta=ad-bc,\quad C=2|\Delta|,\quad
\rho_L=\Psi\Psi^{\mathsf T},\quad
\rho_R=\Psi^{\mathsf T}\Psi.
\]
A nonzero determinant certifies nonfactorization, and equality of both marginals with $I/2$ identifies maximal local mixedness.
\end{algorithm}

This also has $O(1)$ time and space because all matrices have fixed size. For the normalized family
\[
|\psi(\theta)\rangle=\cos\theta|00\rangle+\sin\theta|11\rangle,
\qquad 0\le\theta\le\frac\pi4,
\]
one obtains
\[
C(\theta)=|\sin2\theta|.
\]
The family interpolates from a product state to the Bell state. With binary entropy
\[
H_2(p)=-p\log_2p-(1-p)\log_2(1-p),
\]
the subsystem entropy for $p=\cos^2\theta$ is $H_2(p)$ and the entropy-derived throat is also $H_2(p)$. This is a numerical extension of the central Bell endpoint.

\section{Interpretation and applications}

The identity
\[
w=\frac12 I(L:R)
\]
turns positive mutual information into positive connectivity. The factor $1/2$ arises because the unique throat is counted by each one-sided cut. This resembles inverse problems on weighted graphs: terminal cut measurements encode hidden edge weights. The two-vertex case has one unknown and is exactly solvable; larger trees require more cuts and compatibility conditions.

Bell pairs are resources in teleportation, dense coding, and entanglement swapping. A network whose edges carry such resources suggests a weighted connectivity picture in which one Bell pair provides one normalized unit. Multipartite extensions are not automatic, because pairwise concurrence does not determine general multipartite entanglement and entropy vectors obey nontrivial inequalities.

Tensor networks offer another interpretation. Internal legs organize correlations, while cuts through those legs often bound boundary entanglement. The present geometry is the smallest possible cut network: two boundaries and one connection. It demonstrates how cut data can encode adjacency and weight without introducing unnecessary structure.

\begin{remark}[Physical scope]
A weighted edge is not a Lorentzian spacetime. The model contains no metric tensor, curvature, causal structure, horizon, field equation, semiclassical limit, or gravitational dynamics. It neither predicts traversability nor claims that a laboratory Bell pair creates a macroscopic wormhole. It proves a conditional statement under an explicitly defined cut dictionary.
\end{remark}

\section{Boundary cases and normalization}

If arbitrary input data satisfy $S(L)+S(R)-S(LR)<0$, reconstruction would produce a negative number, which is excluded by the definition of geometry. Such data are rejected rather than interpreted as a negative throat. For genuine quantum entropy, subadditivity makes the corresponding mutual information nonnegative, but the reconstruction theorem itself needs only the stated numerical condition.

Exact inversion is asserted on the generated ray
\[
(S(L),S(R),S(LR))=(w,w,0),\qquad w\ge0.
\]
Away from this ray, the formula still computes half the mutual-information combination, but no claim is made that an arbitrary triple came from a single throat. Unequal one-sided values or nonzero total entropy may require additional degrees of freedom.

The unit bridge is a normalization. Bits give the maximally mixed qubit entropy $1$; natural logarithms give $\ln2$. One could then reconstruct weight $\ln2$ or divide by $\ln2$ to retain unit Bell weight. The invariant content is proportionality between mutual information and the sole geometric parameter.

\section{Discussion and future work}

Three mechanisms drive the correspondence. Matrix rank translates separability into a determinant condition. Contraction translates global amplitudes into local states. Cut inversion translates shared information into geometry. For the Bell state these give, respectively, nonfactorization and unit concurrence, two maximally mixed marginals, and a unit throat.

Several extensions are natural:
\begin{enumerate}
\item allow complex amplitudes and use conjugate transpose in reduced states;
\item define finite-dimensional von Neumann entropy and derive the Bell entropy values spectrally;
\item reconstruct weighted trees from sufficiently rich boundary cut families;
\item introduce finite holographic-code isometries and complementary boundary reconstruction;
\item characterize the entropy vectors produced by nonnegative weighted cuts;
\item study entanglement swapping and possible composition laws for reconstructed throats; and
\item add operator algebras, continuum limits, Lorentzian geometry, and gravitational dynamics needed for comparison with the physical conjecture.
\end{enumerate}

The use of real amplitudes is not essential to the Bell example but limits the state space. In a complex extension, the coefficient determinant remains relevant through its modulus, while reduced matrices use Hermitian conjugation. More substantially, larger geometries introduce inverse problems: boundary data may be insufficient, redundant, or incompatible with nonnegative cuts.

\section{Conclusion}

A real two-qubit Bell state supplies a complete finite example of geometry reconstructed from entanglement. Its coefficient matrix has determinant $1/2$ and concurrence $1$, excluding product factorization. Both reduced density matrices are $I/2$. Its entropy data in bits are $(1,1,0)$, and the two-boundary cut formula maps them to a throat of weight $1$. The same formula exactly recovers every one-throat geometry from the cut data it generates.

The theorem is intentionally narrow. It does not establish the physical ER=EPR conjecture for quantum fields or dynamical spacetimes. It does show, from definitions through proofs and algorithms, how nonfactorization, local mixedness, mutual information, and weighted connectivity can become exact aspects of a single finite system.

